{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>Separate yourselves from the midst of this assembly</b> — One must ask: if Israel did not sin and did not rebel against their master, why was the wrath directed against them, saying \"and I will consume them in a moment\"? And if they too rebelled like Korah and his assembly, how could Moses and Aaron say, \"Shall one man sin and You be wrathful against the whole assembly\"? Now Rabbeinu Chananel wrote: \"Separate yourselves from the midst of this assembly\" — the assembly of Korah, and not the assembly of the children of Israel. \"And they said: El, Elohim of the spirits of all flesh,\" etc. — immediately the Holy One, blessed be He, made known to Moses that He did not seek to destroy the entire assembly of the children of Israel, but only the assembly of Korah. Therefore He explained to him: that which I said, <b>\"Separate yourselves\"</b> — \"Go up from around the dwelling of Korah, Dathan, and Abiram\" — that is what I said to you. He mentioned to them \"go up from around,\" perhaps when Korah and his assembly see it they will turn back. These are his words, and they are not correct, for it would not have been appropriate to say about Korah, Dathan, and Abiram — three men — \"Separate yourselves from the midst of the assembly,\" for they are not an assembly and Israel is not among them, and Aaron was together with the assembly of those offering the incense among them when they came. Furthermore, \"Separate yourselves\" is an allusion to Moses and Aaron alone, like \"Remove yourselves\" (Numbers 17:10), and likewise \"and I will consume them in a moment\" alludes to a plague that would destroy a great and mighty people in an instant. And far be it that Moses should not understand his own prophecy and err in it. Rather, the reason is that at first the heart of the people was with Moses and Aaron, and when Korah and his assembly each took his firepan and put incense upon them and stood at the entrance of the Tent of Meeting with Moses and Aaron, then Korah called to the whole assembly and said to them that it was for the glory of all of them that he was zealous, and the matter seemed good in their eyes and they all gathered to see, perhaps it would be pleasing in the eyes of Elohim and the worship would return to the firstborn. This is the meaning of \"And Korah assembled against them all the assembly\" (Numbers 16:19). Now they had rendered themselves liable to destruction, for they were harboring thoughts against their master, and it is as if they were harboring thoughts against the Shekhinah, and permitting in their hearts the prophecy of a prophet, and they are liable to death by the hand of Heaven. But Moses and Aaron pleaded merit on their behalf, that none had sinned in deed except Korah — and he is the cause and he is the one who enticed them — and it is fitting that he alone die, to make known and publish his punishment to the multitudes. This is the way of those who seek mercy: they lighten the burden of sin from the people and place it upon the individual who caused it, because he is liable in any event. So too David said: \"Behold, I have sinned and I have done wickedly, but these sheep, what have they done? Let Your hand, I pray, be upon me and upon my father's house\" (II Samuel 24:17). Now that punishment upon the people was also for their own sin, for it was incumbent upon them to give the shekels themselves, if that punishment was for this matter according to the words of our Sages (Berakhot 62b), for the king had not commanded them not to give the shekels, for he desired only to know the count, and their punishment and his punishment in this are equal. Furthermore, beyond the census there was a punishment upon the people from the beginning, as it is written: \"And the anger of HaShem burned again against Israel, and He incited David against them\" (II Samuel 24:1). And Rashi wrote there: \"I do not know on what account.\" And I say, by way of reasoning, that there was a punishment upon Israel for the delay in building the Chosen House, for the Ark was going from tent to tent like a stranger in the land, and the tribes were not stirred to say: \"Let us seek HaShem and build the House for His name,\" as it says: \"To His dwelling you shall seek and you shall come there\" (Deuteronomy 12:5). Until David was roused to the matter after many days and a long time, as it says: \"And it came to pass, when the king sat in his house, and HaShem had given him rest from all his enemies around him, that the king said to Nathan the prophet: I dwell in a house of cedar, while the Ark of Elohim dwells within curtains\" (II Samuel 7:1–2). Now HaShem, blessed be He, withheld it from him because He said: \"For you have shed much blood on the earth before Me\" (I Chronicles 22:8), and the building was further delayed until the reign of Solomon. And had Israel desired the matter and been stirred by it from the beginning, it would have been accomplished in the days of one of the Judges, or in the days of Saul, or even in the days of David, for if the tribes of Israel had been stirred to the matter, he would not have been the builder, but Israel would have been the builders. But since the people did not take heed, and David was the one who paid attention and was roused, and he prepared everything, he was the builder. And he is a man of justice who upholds the attribute of strict judgment, and he was not qualified for the House of Mercy. Therefore the building was delayed all the days of David because of Israel's neglect, and therefore the wrath was upon them, and therefore the place that HaShem would choose to set His name there was made known through their punishment and their plague. And Scripture alludes to all this, as it says: \"For I have not dwelt in a house from the day I brought the children of Israel up from Egypt to this day, but I have been walking about,\" etc., \"in all wherever I have walked among all the children of Israel, have I spoken a word with one of the tribes of Israel whom I commanded to shepherd My people Israel, saying: Why have you not built Me a house of cedar?\" (II Samuel 7:6–7) — behold, Scripture reproaches that the Shekhinah walked about among all Israel from tent to tent and from dwelling to dwelling, and not one among all the judges of Israel who shepherded them was stirred to the matter. And Scripture further says that even HaShem kept His distance from them and did not speak to any one of them to build the House, but only now that you were roused — \"you did well, for it was with your heart\" — and now I command to build it through Solomon your son, who will be a man of peace."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:06:40.129915879Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}